        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Communism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1793 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">François-Noël Babeuf, Nicolas Restif de la Bretonne and Sylvain Maréchal, in James H. Billington’s view the progenitors of modern communism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, founders of Marxism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, theorist of the vanguard party; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, who developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>, of permanent revolution; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a>, main theorist of anarcho-communism</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a>’s Utopia (1516), an early picture of common ownership; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> (1848); Friedrich Engels’s The Peasant War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (1850); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>’s Cromwell and Communism (1895); Marx’s Grundrisse and The Civil War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on which libertarian Marxists draw</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">common ownership of the means of production, distribution and exchange; class struggle between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>; historical materialism; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the proletariat as the transition; distribution “from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs”; the end of social classes, money and the state</span></div>
