    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> gave communism its modern definition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> of 1848: a classless society, reached through proletarian revolution, in which goods would be shared according to need.</li><li>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> Communist governments spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, Asia and other regions, and for much of the 20th century more than one-third of the world’s population lived under them.</li><li>Communist states were marked by one-party rule, state control of the economy and the media, and the suppression of opposition; estimates of the deaths under them range from 10–20 million to over 100 million and remain disputed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>One of the first uses of the word in its modern sense is in a letter of around 1785 in which Victor d’Hupay called himself an auteur communiste, a “communist author”.</p></aside>
    </section>

