    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">Nicolas Restif de la Bretonne uses communisme for a social order based on equality and the common ownership of property</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> publish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a>, offering a new definition of communism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> brings Lenin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the first avowedly communist party to take state power</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>, dedicated to promoting revolution throughout the world</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Stalin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> begins; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> trials leading old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> are accused, plead guilty to conspiracy and are executed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> come to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text">Mao’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> begins, a ten-year upheaval that targets intellectuals and party leaders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> is dissolved, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">Revolutions of 1989</a> have brought down most of the Eastern Bloc</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="The Communist Manifesto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist-manifesto.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Friedrich Engels, Karl Marx, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> communis, common. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a> the idea of a classless, egalitarian society first emerged in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> has been considered the first author to give communism serious thought. The 5th-century Mazdak movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, which challenged the privileges of nobles and clergy and the institution of private property, has been called communistic, as have the Waldensians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-muntzer">Thomas Müntzer</a> led a large Anabaptist communist movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants’ War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a> portrayed a society of common property in Utopia (1516), and in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the Diggers called for an end to private ownership of land.</p><p>Criticism of private property continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> with Mably, Meslier, Morelly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>. Victor d’Hupay called himself a communist author around 1785, and in 1793 Restif de la Bretonne used communisme for a social order of equality and common property. In James H. Billington’s view Babeuf, Restif and Sylvain Maréchal, all active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, were the progenitors of modern communism.</p><p>In the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a> founded New Harmony, Indiana, in 1825, and Fourier’s followers founded Brook Farm in 1841. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> advanced, socialist critics blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> for the misery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> of factory workers, and in 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> offered a new definition of communism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/proletariat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proletariat.jpg" alt="Proletariat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">Proletariat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quarto_Stato_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giuseppe Pellizza da Volpedo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Communists hold that the present order stems from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, which divides society into two classes: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, the majority, who must sell their labour power to survive, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, a minority that profits from owning the means of production. The relationship is exploitative and, in their view, can be resolved only by social revolution, which would put the working class in power and establish common ownership. Marxism grounds this in historical materialism, which holds that history is shaped by modes of production and that the change from one to the next is driven by class struggle.</p><p>Between capitalism and communism stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the proletariat, based on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> and meant to be the most democratic of states, with all public authority elected and recallable. A socialist stage, “to each according to his contribution”, would give way, as productive forces advanced, to a classless, stateless society run on the maxim “from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs”. Marxism sets socialisation, control of property by society, above nationalisation, which in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a>’s words does not do away with the capitalist nature of the productive forces.</p><p>Communists disagree on the means. Leninists rely on a vanguard party of professional revolutionaries bound by democratic centralism; libertarian communists favour workers’ councils and self-management; anarcho-communists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a> wanted society to pass at once to communism, without a state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/october-revolution.jpg" alt="October Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:After_the_capture_of_the_Winter_Palace_26_October_1917.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyotr Novitsky, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word fell out of use by the 1860s, and by 1888 Marxists used socialism instead. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 Lenin made socialism a stage between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and communism, and in 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> renamed their party the Communist Party. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>, founded in 1919, carried the Bolshevik model abroad, while council communism, which arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the 1920s, and left communism opposed it.</p><p>After Lenin’s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, resting on dialectical materialism, the leading role of the party and a planned economy with industrialisation and collectivisation, became the official ideology of Communist parties; at its height it covered at least a third of the world’s population. Trotskyism, which upheld permanent and world revolution against Stalin’s socialism in one country, founded the Fourth International in 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were at sword’s point by 1960, splitting the world movement into two hostile camps, and anti-revisionists such as the Maoists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>’s followers rejected the Soviet line after Khrushchev.</p><p>Governments modelled on the Soviet Union took power with Soviet help in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> broke with Stalin in 1948, and the Cuban revolutionaries who overthrew Batista in 1959 became Marxist–Leninist. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> communist parties joined post-war governments and in the 1970s developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurocommunism">Eurocommunism</a>, and since 1957 communists have often won power in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward.jpg" alt="Great Leap Forward" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People%27s_commone_canteen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> gave the land of the church and nobility to the peasants but took their surplus grain, shut down opposing newspapers and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> as a secret police; in January 1918 they dissolved the Constituent Assembly, in which they had won 23.3% of the vote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a>’ 37.6%. War communism forbade strikes and imposed labour duty, and in March 1921 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> crushed a workers’ uprising at Kronstadt. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1936–1938 Stalin set out to destroy any possible opposition in the party, and old Bolsheviks such as Kamenev, Zinoviev, Rykov and Bukharin were tried and executed; the secret police went on sending suspects to the gulag. The state also built a vast research effort, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> launched the world’s first space satellite.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> of 1957–1961 put peasants into large collective farms ordered to start heavy industry; agricultural output fell sharply, causing mass famine and millions of deaths. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> of 1966–1976 millions were accused, humiliated and imprisoned, killed or sent to work as farm labourers. Estimates of the victims of Mao’s government range from 40 to 80 million, through starvation, persecution, prison labour and mass executions; Mao is also praised for advances in literacy, women’s rights, healthcare and life expectancy.</p><p>Estimates of all deaths under Communist states range from 10–20 million to over 100 million; scholars dispute the higher figures and whether the killings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mensheviks.jpg" alt="Mensheviks" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mensevikii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Criticism of communism falls into two kinds: of the practice of 20th-century Communist states, and of Marxist principles and theory. Ludwig von Mises argued that by abolishing free markets, communist officials would lack the price system needed to guide planned production. Planned enterprises hoarded labour and inputs amid chronic shortages and, in the theory of János Kornai, worked under a soft budget constraint; by 1988 output per person in the former planned economies of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was about 43 percent of that in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a> countries. The decline of communism has been attributed to economic inefficiency and to authoritarianism and bureaucracy within its governments.</p><p>Many communists criticised the Soviet path too. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and social democrats thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> too backward for a socialist revolution; council and left communists held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> had built state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, and Trotsky called the Soviet Union under Stalin a degenerated workers’ state.</p><p>The death toll is debated. John Gray, Daniel Goldhagen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a> see communist ideology as a significant cause of mass killings, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> scholars do not consider ideology or regime type an important factor and hold that most Communist states did not carry out mass killings. Mark Bradley wrote that the exact numbers are disputed but the order of magnitude is not. According to Dovid Katz and other historians, a double genocide view equating these deaths with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> is popular in Eastern Europe; some Western scholars reject the comparison.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Public memory of the Communist states has been described as a battleground between anti-communism and anti-anti-communism. The Black Book of Communism, whose figure of 100 million victims is the estimate the Victims of Communism Memorial Foundation most commonly uses, though some of its own authors distanced themselves from it, helped legitimise comparisons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>, and museums and monuments to the victims stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The sociologist András Bozóki set social mobility, the elimination of illiteracy, more accessible healthcare and housing and free higher education against the suppression of freedom, a culture of fear and corruption and closed societies.</p><p>After 1989 and 1991 mortality rose sharply in the former Soviet countries, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> saw life expectancy rise. By 2019 most Eastern Europeans approved of multiparty democracy and a market economy, and 61 percent said living standards were higher than under Communism. Gary Gerstle and Walter Scheidel argue that fear of communism made Western elites readier to compromise with labour, and that the welfare state declined as the threat waned.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s reform and opening up from 1978 brought the poverty rate down from 53% in the Mao era to 8% in 2001, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> have turned toward the market, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> keeps Soviet-style communism.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Communism is the ideology of a classless, stateless society based on common ownership. Given its modern definition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> in 1848, it took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1917 and later ruled more than a third of the world’s population; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party states</a> industrialised and spread literacy, but also carried out purges, famines and repression whose death toll is still debated.</em></p>

